    { label: "The capture of U-110, May 1941", text: "The Royal Navy captured the German submarine U-110 and recovered its Enigma machine and codebooks. Britain kept the capture secret so Germany would not change its codes." },
    { label: "Japanese carriers, November 1941", text: "Japan's carrier force sailed under radio silence. US Navy listening posts, which had tracked those ships by their transmissions, lost track of them. The attack on Pearl Harbor came on December 7." },
    { label: "Station HYPO's estimate, May 1942", text: "Navy codebreakers estimated that the Japanese carriers would arrive from the northwest on about June 4, roughly 175 miles from Midway. Admiral Nimitz placed his own carriers to ambush them." },
    { label: "Operation Vengeance, April 1943", text: "A radio message gave the times and places of Admiral Yamamoto's inspection flight in the Solomon Islands. Navy codebreakers read it, and Admiral Nimitz approved the mission to shoot his plane down." },
    { label: "Operation Fortitude, 1944", text: "The Allies invented an army, led on paper by General Patton, and fed Germany false radio traffic and double-agent reports. Germany believed the main landing would come at Calais, not Normandy." }
